Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Wax Seals Weep Along the Skerry
- Story & Context: When the winter mail cutter ran aground on the black reefs of Bjørnøya in 1998, watchman Einar Holte spent three days hauling waterlogged postal sacks up the lighthouse stairs, discovering bags of personal dispatches that had already spent two years lost in transit. He laid the soaked parchment across the iron warming pipes, the smell of melting red paraffin and damp ink filling the tower as he improvised a heavy, lumbering log-drum rhythm over the shortwave distress channel to guide the stranded crew ashore. Through tears and the wheeze of his salt-pitted harmonica, he broadcast his beacon, whispering into the microphone: "The envelope tore before your ship found the shoal."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Artistic macro shot of a cracked red wax postal seal melting on a frost-covered brass radiator, surrounded by blurred handwritten cursive letters under the blue glow of a lighthouse beam cutting through sea fog.

Track 2: Who Stamps the Mail When the Ice Closes?
- Story & Context: Stationed alone at the Vardø outpost, telecom engineer Sifiso Dlamini kept a bundled packet of unsent letters addressed to his sister in Umlazi, its stamps curled and yellowed by the kerosene heater's dry warmth. During an eighty-day gale that cut off fuel deliveries, he refused to burn the paper for kindling, choosing instead to convert the tower's clockwork pendulum clicks into an undulating, sorrowful Amapiano shuffle that rattled the frost off the south-facing windows. With his fingers stiffening from the biting draft, he blew trembling high notes into his chromatic harmonica, recording a spoken confession into the radio repeater: "Do not read the stamp if you still need the light."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Atmospheric album art featuring a lone kerosene lamp sitting beside a stack of frosted, twine-tied envelopes on a wooden radio desk, with a massive lighthouse lens casting radial sweeps through an icy blizzard outside.

Track 3: Flutter Loss on the Third Dispatch
- Story & Context: Decades after the automated lighthouse at Isfjord was abandoned to the polar gales, a Russian survey vessel intercepted an automated tape loop still broadcasting across emergency marine band 2182 kHz. The reel-to-reel deck had frozen in place during a blizzard in 1993, slowly eating its own magnetic tape and stretching an unmailed telegram of apology into an ethereal, warbling drone beneath a deep, hypnotic log-bass shudder. Marine archivists recovered the loop, finding the weather-ravaged harmonica melody locked into an infinite rhythmic spiral accompanied by a scratchy vocal sample: "Every delayed dispatch lands on an empty hull."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Haunting cinematic view of an abandoned Arctic radio control room encrusted in pale hoarfrost, with magnetic audio tape spilling across rusted metal dials and an icy window showing a frozen ocean under polar twilight.

